      "content": "Escrow, in the context of buying a house, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that the funds are released only when the transaction is complete, rather than being held hostage by the seller. You're right to be confused, but think of it like a protective shield, like a tiny, financial guardian, watching over your money until the deal is sealed.\n\nIn Toronto, where the real estate market can be as unpredictable as a goblin's mood swings, having an escrow account is crucial. It's like having a team of goblin accountants, working tirelessly behind the scenes to ensure that everything is in order.\n\nNow, let's break it down. When you set up an escrow account, you'll typically need to pay a portion of the purchase price upfront, which will be held in the account until the closing date. This is usually around 1-3% of the purchase price, depending on the terms of the sale. The rest of the funds will be released at the closing, when the transaction is complete.\n\nThink of it like a goblin's treasure hoard. You're depositing a portion of your treasure into the escrow account, and it's",
